meta {
  name: Refresh Reused Token (401)
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/auth/refresh
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "refreshToken": "{{refreshToken}}"
  }
}

docs {
  Replay detection. To run it: send Refresh (seq 4) twice in a row WITHOUT
  letting the second run recapture — or just run seq 4, then run this request
  with the token seq 4 consumed.

  Expect 401 {"error": "Invalid refresh token"}.

  Then confirm the side effect in SQL — this is the half Bruno cannot assert:

    SELECT revoked_reason, count(*) FROM sessions
    WHERE family_id = '<the family>' GROUP BY revoked_reason;

  Every row in that family must now read 'reuse_detected'. A stolen refresh
  token is only useful until the real client next refreshes; that collision is
  what burns the family for both of them.

  Caveat: a replay within 30 seconds of the original rotation is treated as an
  honest retry (a lost response), not an attack, and returns 200. Wait out the
  grace window before expecting the 401.
}
